WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.160
Most sensory receptors are excited by their stimulus.

2
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.140
Vertebrate photoreceptors are turned off by theirs.

3
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.080
A rod sitting in the dark is depolarized.

4
00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.000
Current flows steadily into it, and it releases glutamate all the time.

5
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.240
Light makes it stop.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.200
How does turning a cell off let you see?

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:33.663
In darkness, cyclic GMP holds CNG channels open in the outer segment.

8
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:36.763
Sodium and calcium flow in.

9
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:42.883
That steady dark current keeps the rod depolarized, so it releases glutamate.

10
00:00:44.123 --> 00:00:47.063
Now a photon is absorbed by rhodopsin.

11
00:00:47.823 --> 00:00:51.443
One activated rhodopsin activates many transducins.

12
00:00:52.403 --> 00:00:57.643
Transducin activates PDE6, which breaks down cyclic GMP.

13
00:00:58.423 --> 00:01:01.163
The channels close, and the dark current shrinks.

14
00:01:02.023 --> 00:01:05.363
The rod hyperpolarizes, and glutamate release falls.

15
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.753
The rod releases one transmitter, glutamate.

16
00:01:10.453 --> 00:01:13.793
It releases more in the dark and less in the light.

17
00:01:14.653 --> 00:01:19.853
Yet some bipolar cells depolarize to light, and others hyperpolarize.

18
00:01:20.793 --> 00:01:24.553
Glutamate's sign is set by the receptor, not by the molecule.

19
00:01:25.793 --> 00:01:29.153
OFF bipolar cells use AMPA or kainate receptors.

20
00:01:29.793 --> 00:01:34.053
In the dark, glutamate opens cation channels and depolarizes them.

21
00:01:34.053 --> 00:01:38.133
Light removes that excitation, so they hyperpolarize.

22
00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:39.853
The sign is preserved.

23
00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:46.103
ON bipolar cells use a different receptor, mGluR6.

24
00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:53.103
In the dark, glutamate on mGluR6 activates the G protein Go.

25
00:01:53.763 --> 00:01:57.423
Go keeps the channel TRPM1 closed.

26
00:01:58.643 --> 00:01:59.823
Light lowers glutamate.

27
00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:04.103
Go activity declines, and TRPM1 opens.

28
00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:06.383
The cell depolarizes.

29
00:02:06.963 --> 00:02:08.303
The sign is inverted.

30
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:16.097
So each photoreceptor feeds two parallel streams, one signalling lighter and one

31
00:02:16.097 --> 00:02:17.097
signalling darker.

32
00:02:18.177 --> 00:02:21.197
ON bipolar cells drive ON ganglion cells.

33
00:02:21.977 --> 00:02:24.837
OFF bipolar cells drive OFF ganglion cells.

34
00:02:25.897 --> 00:02:31.277
In cat retina, light on the centre of a field makes an ON centre ganglion cell fire

35
00:02:31.277 --> 00:02:33.977
faster, and suppresses an OFF centre cell.

36
00:02:33.977 --> 00:02:37.637
Ganglion cells fire action potentials.

37
00:02:37.837 --> 00:02:39.497
They are the retina's only output.

38
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:46.290
Light switches the photoreceptor off, and the retina reads that silence in two

39
00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:50.550
opposite ways. OFF bipolar cells keep the sign.

40
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.490
ON bipolar cells invert it.

41
00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.830
Keep this rule. The receptor, not the molecule, sets the sign.
